.Dd September 23, 2026
.Dt PAZARI 7
.Os pazari
.Sh NAME
.Nm pazari
.Nd guest-initiated memory negotiation for virtual machines
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Virtual machines are usually sized by guesswork: a static allocation that is
either wasteful or too small, chosen before anyone knows what the workload will
do.
Under
.Nm
the guest decides when it needs memory instead.
From outside the VM a workload growing into its working set and a workload
thrashing look the same; from inside they do not.
.Pp
The name is the Greek word for haggling.
.Pp
Three programs make it up:
.Bl -tag -width "pazari-stress" -offset indent
.It Xr pazari-agent 1
runs inside the guest.
It brakes one cgroup with
.Va memory.high
instead of letting the kernel kill what is in it, and asks the host for memory
when the brake bites.
.It Xr pazarid 1
runs on the host.
It arbitrates those requests across every guest it serves and actuates a grant
by resizing that guest's virtio-mem device over QMP.
.It Xr pazari-stress 1
is a deterministic memory workload, for measuring any of the above.
.El
.Pp
The agent and the daemon talk over a newline-delimited JSON protocol carried on
a vsock or unix stream.
Every message carries absolute values.
Neither side keeps state across a connection, and there is no error message: a
peer that reads something it does not understand closes the connection, and the
guest reconnects.
.Sh HOW A GROW HAPPENS
.Bl -enum
.It
The workload grows into the band between
.Va memory.high
and
.Va memory.max .
The kernel throttles it at
.Va memory.high
and raises the cgroup's own throttle counter.
.It
The counter wakes the agent, which opens the band: it moves
.Va memory.high
up to within
.Cm open_guard
of
.Va memory.max ,
so the workload may burst instead of stalling for the whole round trip, and
sends a grant request.
.It
The daemon decides.
If the host has the memory, less what is already promised to the other guests
and less its own floor, it answers with a grant and asks the hypervisor to make
the device larger.
.It
The device grows.
The guest's kernel onlines the new memory, the guest's
.Va MemTotal
rises, and a uevent wakes the agent.
.It
The agent re-places the band against the larger guest and goes back to steady.
.El
.Pp
Shrinking runs the same way backwards, and is always the guest's idea: a
workload that has been idle for long enough makes the agent reclaim memory out
of its cgroup and offer it back.
The host may ask for memory back when it is itself under pressure, but it
cannot take it.
.Pp
How long step 3 may take is set by
.Cm open_guard ,
the memory kept between the open band and
.Va memory.max .
The kernel throttles the workload as it climbs through that memory, and the
climb is what the grant has to arrive within.
.Xr pazari-agent.toml 5
carries the measurements behind it.
.Sh DEPLOYMENT
Both programs are ordinary system daemons that read one configuration file.
What takes setting up is around them: a memory device for the guest to grow
into, a control channel the host daemon may use, and a cgroup the agent is
allowed to brake.
.Ss Giving a guest a device to grow into
The guest needs a virtio-mem device with room in it.
With qemu:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
qemu-system-x86_64 \e
    -machine q35 \e
    -m 2048M,maxmem=32768M \e
    -object memory-backend-ram,id=vmem0,size=30720M \e
    -device virtio-mem-pci,id=vm0,memdev=vmem0,requested-size=0 \e
    ...
.Ed
.Pp
The backend's
.Va size
is the whole capacity the guest may grow into, and
.Va maxmem
must cover the boot memory plus that.
.Va requested-size
is what the device starts plugged with; the daemon moves it from there.
.Va id
is what names the device in
.Xr pazarid.toml 5 .
.Pp
The machine model must carry PCI, so qemu's own
.Va microvm
model will not do: it has no virtio-mem.
.Pp
Leave the device's block size alone.
Moving it buys nothing measurable: a guest run with the device at 128MiB blocks
instead of the 2MiB default measured the same median grow round trip to within
six milliseconds, over ten grows each, and gave back exactly as much memory
when it went idle.
It can cost something.
The agent rounds its targets to the guest kernel's memory block size, 128MiB on
x86-64, which is a different number and is not configurable; a device block
size above it would leave the daemon asking for sizes the hypervisor refuses,
which it counts as
.Va pazarid_device_refused_actuations_total .
.Ss A control channel of the daemon's own
The daemon drives the device over QMP, and holds its connection open.
Most things that run VMs already drive a QMP socket of their own, so give the
daemon a second one rather than have the two of them share:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
    -qmp unix:/run/pazari/example.qmp,server=on,wait=off
.Ed
.Pp
Contention is only part of the reason.
A QMP socket is the hypervisor's whole control channel, and anything that can
talk to it can do far more than resize a memory device.
A socket of the daemon's own means its access can be reasoned about, and taken
away, without touching what the VM manager needs.
.Pp
The daemon needs no privilege beyond reaching that socket, which is a matter of
file ownership: run it as its own user, put that user in the group the socket
belongs to, and create the socket with a group-readable mask.
.Pp
Where guests reach the daemon over vsock, the host needs
.Va vhost_vsock
loaded.
.Ss Booting the guest
The agent refuses to start on a guest that cannot be braked safely.
Boot with:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
transparent_hugepage=madvise psi=1
.Ed
.Pp
Give the guest no virtio-balloon.
A balloon and an agent both move the memory the guest has, and preflight
refuses to start against one.
.Pp
.Nm pazari-agent Cm preflight
says what each of these is for and what else it checks.
.Ss The units
The agent brakes one cgroup and must not be in it.
Give the workload a slice of its own, keep anything watching memory pressure
off that slice, and run the agent under
.Va system.slice :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# workload.slice
[Slice]
MemoryAccounting=yes
ManagedOOMMemoryPressure=auto
ManagedOOMSwap=auto
ManagedOOMPreference=omit
.Ed
.Pp
A braked workload sits at
.Va memory.high
and stalls there on purpose, which is indistinguishable from a workload in
trouble to anything watching pressure.
.Va ManagedOOMPreference=omit
is the directive that matters; the other two only decline to opt in.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# pazari-agent.service
[Service]
Type=notify
ExecStart=/usr/bin/pazari-agent --config /etc/pazari/agent.toml
ExecStopPost=/usr/bin/pazari-agent panic-open --config /etc/pazari/agent.toml
WatchdogSec=30s
Restart=always
RestartSec=5s
Slice=system.slice
.Ed
.Pp
Do not leave out the
.Va ExecStopPost .
An agent that is killed outright leaves the workload braked at limits nothing
will move again;
.Cm panic-open
takes the brake off after every exit, orderly or not.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# pazarid.service
[Service]
Type=notify
ExecStart=/usr/bin/pazarid --config /etc/pazari/pazarid.toml
WatchdogSec=30s
Restart=always
RestartSec=5s
User=pazarid
SupplementaryGroups=kvm
.Ed
.Ss Writing the two configurations
.Xr pazarid.toml 5
and
.Xr pazari-agent.toml 5
document every setting and carry a worked example each.
Either program will print an annotated file at its defaults to start from:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
pazarid example-config > /etc/pazari/pazarid.toml
pazari-agent example-config > /etc/pazari/agent.toml
.Ed
.Sh PUTTING THE WORKLOAD IN THE CGROUP
Memory the agent cannot see is memory it cannot negotiate for.
Every workload the guest exists to run belongs in the slice the agent brakes.
What puts it there is whatever creates its cgroup, which is not always the unit
that was declared.
.Pp
A quadlet container is its unit.
Quadlet runs podman at
.Va CgroupsMode=split ,
which puts the container's payload in a sub-cgroup of the unit's own, so the
unit's slice is the container's slice.
Set
.Va Slice=workload.slice
on the unit and the payload follows.
.Pp
A container that some daemon starts on the workload's behalf is not.
Where the workload holds a docker or podman socket and asks for containers
through it, which is what CI runners do, the daemon on the other end creates
them under its own default parent.
.Pp
Docker on a cgroup v2 host drives systemd and parents its scopes at
.Va system.slice ,
so those containers land as
.Pa system.slice/docker-<id>.scope :
charged to the guest's own services, watched by whatever manages
.Va system.slice ,
and invisible to the agent.
Point the daemon at the slice instead, with
.Va cgroup-parent
in
.Pa /etc/docker/daemon.json
or the equivalent for whatever creates the containers.
.Pp
The daemon itself stays out of the slice.
It is a service of the guest rather than of the workload, and what it holds
belongs in
.Cm engine.service_reserve .
.Sh OBSERVING
Both programs publish OpenMetrics over HTTP when their
.Va metrics
table is configured; the series are listed in
.Xr pazari-agent.toml 5
and
.Xr pazarid.toml 5 .
.Pp
The four to watch on a host that is behaving:
.Va pazarid_host_available_bytes
against
.Cm policy.host_floor ,
.Va pazarid_denies_total
broken out by reason,
.Va pazari_agent_mode
for how long a guest sits in
.Cm awaiting_grant ,
and
.Va pazari_agent_max_events ,
which should stay at zero, because a workload that reached
.Va memory.max
is a workload the brake did not catch.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa /etc/pazari/agent.toml
The agent's configuration.
See
.Xr pazari-agent.toml 5 .
.It Pa /etc/pazari/pazarid.toml
The daemon's configuration.
See
.Xr pazarid.toml 5 .
.It Pa share/doc/pazari/examples
Annotated configuration files for both, as installed with the package.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr pazari-agent 1 ,
.Xr pazari-stress 1 ,
.Xr pazarid 1 ,
.Xr pazari-agent.toml 5 ,
.Xr pazarid.toml 5
.Pp
.Xr cgroups 7 ,
.Xr systemd-oomd 8
